  "metadata": {
    "title": "2 SpaceX retail investors explain why they bought the IPO & still want more shares",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Two SpaceX (SPCX) retail investors who bought shares at the initial public offering (IPO), Tamar June and Shane Williams, join Yahoo Finance to explain the process, what drew them to the company, and their future plans with the stock.\nWe're joined now by two of SpaceX's retail investor base who did get some shares. Just talk us through the process and how it worked for you.\nI was told right off the bat that you may not get what you actually want, you know, I I requested 1400 and I ended up with 600 at uh the night before.\nAnd uh I was happy with that. I knew it was oversubscribed.\nI saw that it was way over subscribed on the retail side for sure. Um something like a 100 billion dollars and it was a 75 billion dollar IPO to begin with.\nAnd uh I I was happy with it. I didn't get nearly as many shares as I wanted.\nWhat drew you to this? Are you someone who's invested in Tesla, for example? Are you like an Elon Musk guy?\nFor sure an Elon Musk guy. I mean, obviously he's the greatest inventor in our our lifetime possibly even humanity.\nThey're obviously aspiring to do things that have never been done before. They're currently already doing things that have never been done before and so I think if you just extrapolate that success out into the future, it lends itself to to what you would expect to be a great opportunity.\nActually, I got into SpaceX back in 2020 and again in 2023 through an SPV. Uh, I am subject to that lockup period, obviously.\nI've been, you know, interested in space technology for quite some time and, you know, looking at how you know, Starlink came about and the explosive growth and the opportunity in the future for, you know, capturing pot potentially 20% of the broadband market.\nPlus the other uh area that I'm very interested in is the satellite to cell technology, which I'm hoping will come into real fruition where, you know, there won't be any more dead zones and we'll be able to have, you know, excellent coverage wherever you go.\nNow that the stock's public, are you planning to pick some more up? Are you waiting for it to maybe fall a little bit before you would get in?\nFor sure pick more up. Um we'll we'll see what the price does. It it'll most likely be volatile here in the short term. So if it does dip for sure and then even if it doesn't dip because I think long-term it, you know, it's still cheap to be honest.\nI'm going to observe. I'm a long-term investor to begin with. So, uh if there is another opportunity to pick them up, I will probably do so, but, you know, I'm also holding out for uh more IPOs this year.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "Two SpaceX (SPCX) retail investors who bought shares at the initial public offering (IPO), Tamar June and Shane Williams, join Yahoo Finance to explain the process, what drew them to the company, and their future plans with the stock.",
      "We're joined now by two of SpaceX's retail investor base who did get some shares. Just talk us through the process and how it worked for you.",
      "I was told right off the bat that you may not get what you actually want, you know, I I requested 1400 and I ended up with 600 at uh the night before.",
      "And uh I was happy with that. I knew it was oversubscribed.",
      "I saw that it was way over subscribed on the retail side for sure. Um something like a 100 billion dollars and it was a 75 billion dollar IPO to begin with.",
      "And uh I I was happy with it. I didn't get nearly as many shares as I wanted.",
      "What drew you to this? Are you someone who's invested in Tesla, for example? Are you like an Elon Musk guy?",
      "For sure an Elon Musk guy. I mean, obviously he's the greatest inventor in our our lifetime possibly even humanity.",
      "They're obviously aspiring to do things that have never been done before. They're currently already doing things that have never been done before and so I think if you just extrapolate that success out into the future, it lends itself to to what you would expect to be a great opportunity.",
      "Actually, I got into SpaceX back in 2020 and again in 2023 through an SPV. Uh, I am subject to that lockup period, obviously.",
      "I've been, you know, interested in space technology for quite some time and, you know, looking at how you know, Starlink came about and the explosive growth and the opportunity in the future for, you know, capturing pot potentially 20% of the broadband market.",
      "Plus the other uh area that I'm very interested in is the satellite to cell technology, which I'm hoping will come into real fruition where, you know, there won't be any more dead zones and we'll be able to have, you know, excellent coverage wherever you go.",
      "Now that the stock's public, are you planning to pick some more up? Are you waiting for it to maybe fall a little bit before you would get in?",
      "For sure pick more up. Um we'll we'll see what the price does. It it'll most likely be volatile here in the short term. So if it does dip for sure and then even if it doesn't dip because I think long-term it, you know, it's still cheap to be honest.",
      "I'm going to observe. I'm a long-term investor to begin with. So, uh if there is another opportunity to pick them up, I will probably do so, but, you know, I'm also holding out for uh more IPOs this year."
